16. AI BUSINESS ASSISTANT

Create a dedicated AI assistant page.

The assistant should help business owners understand their own business data.

Example questions:

- How much did I sell this month?
- Which products are running low?
- Which customers have overdue balances?
- What were my largest expense categories?
- Which products sold the most last week?
- How much money have I collected this month?
- Which invoices need attention?

Architecture:

- Use a server-side AI integration.
- Keep API keys on the server.
- Define strict access controls.
- Retrieve only data belonging to the authenticated business.
- Validate tool arguments.
- Restrict the assistant to permitted business operations.
- Apply rate limits and usage quotas.
- Provide useful error handling.

The AI assistant must not have unrestricted SQL access.

Implement controlled server-side functions for supported queries.

Use structured outputs when appropriate.

Do not allow model-generated instructions to bypass authentication, business isolation, or permissions.

The assistant must distinguish between:

- Actual recorded facts
- Calculated estimates
- Recommendations
- Missing information

If the data is unavailable, the assistant should say so.

Never fabricate sales, customers, products, balances, or business performance.

For recommendations, clearly label them as suggestions rather than guaranteed business outcomes.

Do not send invoices, change records, delete transactions, or trigger payments through AI without an explicit user action and appropriate confirmation.

If an AI API key is unavailable, show a clear configuration message and keep the rest of the application functional.

Do not pretend the AI assistant is connected when it is not.

